    <div class="container">
      <div class="badge-row">
        <span class="badge badge-accent">DARTS ESSENTIALS</span>
        <span class="badge">A-Z</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Darts Glossary and Slang (A-Z)</h1>
      <p class="lead-text">Professional darts possesses one of the richest, most colourful vocabularies in world sport. Born in centuries-old British public houses and elevated onto global television stages by iconic commentators like Sid Waddell and John Gwynne, darts terminology combines razor-sharp mathematical precision with playful pub folklore.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="container content-layout">
    <main class="main-column">
      <VerificationBadge verifiedDate="October 2026" topic="Darts Terminology" />

      <section class="content-card">

<p>Whether you are watching the PDC World Darts Championship at Alexandra Palace, calculating your outshot at a local league match, or learning why hitting the wire is called a "wire bounce," this definitive directory explains every vital term, rule, and piece of slang from A to Z.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Quick Navigation: Jump to Letter</h2>
<p><a href="#letter-a">A</a> | <a href="#letter-b">B</a> | <a href="#letter-c">C</a> | <a href="#letter-d">D</a> | <a href="#letter-e">E</a> | <a href="#letter-f">F</a> | <a href="#letter-g">G</a> | <a href="#letter-h">H</a> | <a href="#letter-i">I</a> | <a href="#letter-j">J</a> | <a href="#letter-k">K</a> | <a href="#letter-l">L</a> | <a href="#letter-m">M</a> | <a href="#letter-n">N</a> | <a href="#letter-o">O</a> | <a href="#letter-p">P</a> | <a href="#letter-q">Q</a> | <a href="#letter-r">R</a> | <a href="#letter-s">S</a> | <a href="#letter-t">T</a> | <a href="#letter-u">U</a> | <a href="#letter-v">V</a> | <a href="#letter-w">W</a> | <a href="#letter-x">X</a> | <a href="#letter-y">Y</a> | <a href="#letter-z">Z</a></p>
      </section>
      <AdSlot position="in-article" />
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>A-Z term index (term / 1-line definition / 'Full guide' link where one exists)</h2>
<p>Below is the master index of over 85 professional and grassroots darts terms. Every entry provides a concise, plain-English definition, with links to dedicated guides for in-depth rules and strategy.</p>
<h3>Letter A</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-a"></a><strong>Aiming:</strong> The visual alignment of the dominant eye, dart tip, and target bed prior to initiating the backward draw. See our <a href="/darts/how-to-throw-darts/">How to Throw Darts Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Ally Pally:</strong> The affectionate colloquial name for Alexandra Palace in North London, the permanent home of the PDC World Darts Championship since December 2007.</li>
<li><strong>Annie's Room:</strong> Traditional British pub slang for <strong>Double 1</strong>, the lowest scoring double on the board (synonymous with <em>The Madhouse</em>).</li>
<li><strong>Around the Clock:</strong> A classic foundational practice drill where a player throws sequentially at numbers 1 through 20 before finishing on the bullseye. Read our <a href="/darts/practice-routines/">Practice Routines Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Arrows:</strong> Universal popular slang term for darts.</li>
<li><strong>Averages (3-Dart):</strong> The primary statistical metric measuring the average score per visit of three darts thrown throughout a leg or match. Learn the formula in our <a href="/darts/average-explained/">3-Dart Average Guide</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter B</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-b"></a><strong>Baby Fish:</strong> Slang for a checkout of <strong>130</strong>, typically completed via Treble 20, Treble 18, and Double 8, referencing a junior sibling to the 170 "Big Fish".</li>
<li><strong>Bag of Nails:</strong> Traditional pub term for scoring <strong>26 points</strong> in a single visit (Single 20, Single 1, Single 5) when aiming for the treble 20.</li>
<li><strong>Barn Dart:</strong> The third and final dart of a three-dart turn, especially when it salvages an otherwise poor visit or hits a critical target under pressure.</li>
<li><strong>Barney Army:</strong> The worldwide fan contingent and signature chant that accompanied five-time World Champion Raymond van Barneveld throughout his career. See our <a href="/fan-culture/chants-songs/">Darts Chants &amp; Fan Songs Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Barrel:</strong> The central metal body of the dart held by the player, commonly manufactured from dense tungsten nickel alloys for slim profiles and tight grouping.</li>
<li><strong>Bed:</strong> Any discrete scoring section bounded by metal wires (spider) on a regulation dartboard.</li>
<li><strong>Bed and Breakfast:</strong> British slang for a 3-dart score of <strong>26</strong> (traditionally 2 shillings and 6 pence, the historic cost of a modest bed-and-breakfast stay).</li>
<li><strong>Big Fish:</strong> The affectionate nickname for a <strong>170 checkout</strong>—the absolute maximum finish in 501 darts (Treble 20, Treble 20, Bullseye). Explore why in our <a href="/darts/why-is-170-the-highest-checkout/">170 Checkout Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Blind 180:</strong> A maximum score of 180 achieved when earlier darts physically or visually obscure the treble bed from the player's view at the oche.</li>
<li><strong>Board Height:</strong> The official regulatory vertical distance from the center of the bullseye to the floor, strictly set at <strong>5 feet 8 inches (1.73 meters)</strong>. See our <a href="/darts/board-height-and-distance/">Board Height &amp; Distance Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Bogey Numbers:</strong> Seven mathematical totals below 170 (<strong>169, 168, 166, 165, 163, 162, and 159</strong>) that are mathematically impossible to check out in three darts under double-out rules. Master them in our <a href="/darts/bogey-numbers-in-darts/">Bogey Numbers Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Bounce-Out:</strong> A dart that strikes the wire, barrel, or sisal and falls to the floor before extraction; under DRA rules, bounce-outs score zero points.</li>
<li><strong>Break of Throw:</strong> Winning a leg in which the opposing player threw first, equivalent to a break of serve in tennis.</li>
<li><strong>Bristle Board:</strong> A competition dartboard made of tightly compressed sisal fibers (originating from agave plants) that close up when darts are withdrawn.</li>
<li><strong>Bull-Off (Corking):</strong> The pre-match ritual where each player throws one dart at the bullseye to determine who throws first in the opening leg.</li>
<li><strong>Bullseye (Inner Bull):</strong> The small red circular zone at the exact center of the dartboard, measuring 12.7mm across, worth <strong>50 points</strong>, and legally counting as a double.</li>
<li><strong>Bust Rule:</strong> The rule specifying that if a player scores more than their remaining total, leaves exactly 1, or reaches zero without a double, the visit is void and their score resets. Read our <a href="/darts/bust-rule-in-darts/">Bust Rule Guide</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter C</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-c"></a><strong>Caller:</strong> The licensed tournament referee (such as Kirk Bevins, George Noble, or Russ Bray) who calls out scores to the arena crowd and broadcast audience.</li>
<li><strong>Chalker (Marker):</strong> The official or volunteer responsible for recording scores, calculating remaining totals, and updating the scoreboard.</li>
<li><strong>Champagne Breakfast:</strong> Humorous pub slang for hitting three Triple 1s in a single visit, yielding a meager total of <strong>9 points</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Checkout:</strong> The final scoring shot or combination that reduces a player's remaining score from an outshot to zero, concluding on a double or bullseye. Reference our <a href="/darts/checkout-chart/">PDC Checkout Chart</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Checkout Percentage:</strong> A statistical metric calculating the ratio of successful checkout doubles hit relative to total finishing double attempts.</li>
<li><strong>Choke:</strong> Missing high-leverage match darts or finishing doubles due to intense psychological pressure.</li>
<li><strong>Circuit:</strong> The complete annual calendar of floor events, European Tour stages, and televised majors organized by the PDC.</li>
<li><strong>Clamping:</strong> An error in throwing technique where a player squeezes the dart barrel excessively tight, disrupting release consistency.</li>
<li><strong>Closing:</strong> In American/Cricket darts, hitting a number three times (via singles, doubles, or trebles) to make it eligible for point-scoring. Learn the rules in our <a href="/darts/how-to-play-cricket/">How to Play Cricket Darts Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Cork:</strong> An alternative, traditional American term for the Bullseye, derived from historic pub targets made from barrel corks.</li>
<li><strong>Cricket:</strong> The popular alternative darts game based on claiming numbers 15 through 20 and the Bullseye before accumulating points against an opponent. See our <a href="/darts/how-to-play-cricket/">Cricket Rules Guide</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter D</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-d"></a><strong>Dartitis:</strong> A severe psychological motor condition akin to the "yips" in golf, causing an involuntary mental block or freeze that prevents a smooth release.</li>
<li><strong>Dead Eye:</strong> Scoring three consecutive 50-point Bullseyes in a single three-dart visit, totaling 150 points.</li>
<li><strong>Decider:</strong> The final leg of a match or set when the score is tied, played as sudden death.</li>
<li><strong>Deflection:</strong> When an incoming dart strikes an embedded dart in the board, changing its flight path into another bed, into the wire, or onto the floor.</li>
<li><strong>Double:</strong> The outer narrow ring on the dartboard, measuring 8mm wide, which doubles the numerical point value of the segment (e.g., Double 20 = 40 points).</li>
<li><strong>Double Bull:</strong> Another common term for the 50-point central Bullseye, distinguishing it from the 25-point outer bull.</li>
<li><strong>Double-In Double-Out (DIDO):</strong> A demanding format where a player must hit a double to begin scoring in a leg and a double to finish (standard at the PDC World Grand Prix). See our <a href="/darts/double-in-double-out/">Double-In Double-Out Explainer</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Double Trouble:</strong> A match passage where both players repeatedly miss attempts at a finishing double across multiple visits, driving down averages.</li>
<li><strong>Downstairs:</strong> Commentary slang referring to the lower half of the dartboard, especially the <strong>Treble 19</strong> segment (57 points), used when Treble 20 is obstructed.</li>
<li><strong>DRA:</strong> The <strong>Darts Regulation Authority</strong>, the independent governing body establishing rules, equipment standards, and disciplinary codes for professional darts.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter E</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-e"></a><strong>Elbow Alignment:</strong> A foundational element of throwing mechanics where the elbow stays aligned vertically and horizontally with the target throughout the delivery.</li>
<li><strong>Exhibition:</strong> A non-ranking showcase event where professional players entertain audiences with trick shots, fan interaction, and informal matches.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter F</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-f"></a><strong>First-9 Average:</strong> A specialized performance metric evaluating a player's scoring average across only the first nine darts (three visits) of each leg, reflecting pure power scoring before doubles attempts.</li>
<li><strong>Fish &amp; Chips:</strong> British slang for scoring <strong>33 points</strong> in a visit (Single 20, Single 12, Single 1).</li>
<li><strong>Flight:</strong> The plastic, nylon, or composite aerodynamic wing fitted into the shaft that stabilizes the dart's flight path.</li>
<li><strong>Floor Tournament:</strong> Non-televised ranking events (such as PDC Players Championship days) staged across dozens of boards simultaneously in arena halls.</li>
<li><strong>Follow-Through:</strong> The natural physical extension of the throwing hand and arm toward the target board after the dart has left the fingers.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter G</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-g"></a><strong>Game On:</strong> The traditional vocal declaration made by the referee or Master of Ceremonies to initiate a leg or match.</li>
<li><strong>Game Shot:</strong> The formal call shouted by the referee when a competitor successfully lands their final checkout double to claim a leg, set, or match.</li>
<li><strong>Grouping:</strong> The physical proximity of all three darts when embedded in the board, demonstrating mechanical repeatability in delivery.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter H</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-h"></a><strong>Half a Crown:</strong> Traditional pub slang for scoring <strong>26 points</strong> (two shillings and sixpence in pre-decimal British coinage).</li>
<li><strong>Hat-Trick:</strong> Landing all three darts of a turn in the central Bullseye (50 points each), scoring 150 points.</li>
<li><strong>Hold of Throw:</strong> Winning a leg in which you threw first, protecting the mathematical advantage of opening the scoring.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter I</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-i"></a><strong>Inner Bull:</strong> The central 50-point red disc (Bullseye).</li>
<li><strong>Island:</strong> The active scoring surface of the dartboard enclosed within the outer double wire.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter J</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-j"></a><strong>Junior Darts Corporation (JDC):</strong> The worldwide junior development organization providing formal tournament pathways and coaching academies for players aged 10 to 18.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter K</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-k"></a><strong>Key Checkouts:</strong> Crucial combination outshots (such as 121, 81, or 64) that offer multiple tactical paths to a double while leaving clean backup targets if a treble is missed. Use our <a href="/tools/checkout-calculator/">Checkout Calculator Tool</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter L</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-l"></a><strong>Leg:</strong> A single game of 501. The basic scoring unit of darts; the first competitor to reduce 501 to zero on a double wins the leg.</li>
<li><strong>Legs vs Sets:</strong> The two primary tournament matchplay structures. Legs-only play awards victory to the first player to reach a target leg count; set play groups legs into sets (usually best of 5 legs per set). Compare them in our <a href="/darts/legs-vs-sets/">Legs vs Sets Guide</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter M</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-m"></a><strong><a href="/darts/madhouse-in-darts/">Madhouse:</a></strong> Universal darts slang for <strong>Double 1</strong> (worth 2 points). It is notoriously difficult because any inside miss into the single 1 busts the score to zero.</li>
<li><strong>Marker (Dart):</strong> A dart intentionally positioned slightly above or below a target wire to serve as a physical backstop or sight-line guide for subsequent throws.</li>
<li><strong>Master of Ceremonies (MC):</strong> The stage host (such as John McDonald) who introduces competitors, announces walk-ons, and conducts on-stage trophy presentations.</li>
<li><strong>Maximum:</strong> A score of <strong>180 points</strong> achieved by placing all three darts cleanly into the Treble 20 bed in a single turn. Read our complete <a href="/darts/what-is-a-180-in-darts/">180 Maximum Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Mugging:</strong> A casual tavern rule where the loser of a game pays for the round of drinks or chalks the next contest.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter N</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-n"></a><strong>Nine-Dart Finish (Nine-Darter):</strong> The perfect leg in 501 darts, requiring a player to check out using the absolute mathematical minimum of nine darts. Explore the history in our <a href="/darts/what-is-a-9-dart-finish/">Nine-Dart Finish Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Nine-Dart Romance:</strong> A colorful phrase coined by commentator Sid Waddell describing the electric tension when a player steps up to throw for a nine-darter.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter O</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-o"></a><strong>Oche (The Throw Line):</strong> The raised barrier or line behind which a player must stand. The official regulatory distance from the face of the board to the back edge of the oche is <strong>7 feet 9¼ inches (2.37 meters)</strong>. See our <a href="/darts/board-height-and-distance/">Board Height &amp; Distance Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Order of Merit:</strong> The official PDC ranking ledger, ranking professional players by prize money earned over a rolling two-year period in ranking tournaments.</li>
<li><strong>Outer Bull:</strong> The green circular band surrounding the Bullseye, measuring 31.8mm across, worth <strong>25 points</strong>, and treated as a single for scoring purposes.</li>
<li><strong>Outshot:</strong> Another term for a checkout; the successful completion of a leg on a double.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter P</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-p"></a><strong>Pencil Grip:</strong> A throwing technique where the player holds the barrel between thumb and index finger like a writing pen.</li>
<li><strong>Pie:</strong> American slang for any single triangular scoring segment on the dartboard.</li>
<li><strong>Points:</strong> The steel or soft tips protruding from the front of the barrel that penetrate the dartboard.</li>
<li><strong>Pressure Double:</strong> A high-leverage checkout attempt taken when the opponent is sitting on a finish, where a miss likely concedes the leg.</li>
<li><strong>ProTour:</strong> The circuit of non-televised PDC ranking floor tournaments, comprising Players Championship days and European Tour qualifiers.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter Q</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-q"></a><strong>Q-School (Qualifying School):</strong> The grueling annual PDC qualifying event held each January in the UK and Europe, where hundreds of players compete to win two-year PDC Tour Cards.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter R</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-r"></a><strong>Right Side:</strong> Shifting aim to numbers on the right half of the board (e.g., 18, 4, 13, 6, 10, 15, 2) when the 20 and 19 beds are congested.</li>
<li><strong>Robin Hood:</strong> The rare event where a dart's steel point lodges into the flight or shaft of a dart already in the board. Under DRA rules, a Robin Hood scores <strong>zero points</strong> because the point does not contact the board. Read our <a href="/darts/robin-hood-in-darts/">Robin Hood In Darts Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Round:</strong> A single visit consisting of three darts thrown by one player.</li>
<li><strong>Round the Clock:</strong> Another common name for the sequential 1-through-20 practice routine.</li>
<li><strong>Route:</strong> The tactical sequence of segments selected by a player to reach their preferred finishing double.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter S</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-s"></a><strong>Scorer:</strong> The electronic terminal or software system operated by official markers to log scores and generate broadcast statistics in real time.</li>
<li><strong>Scoreboard:</strong> The physical chalk board, whiteboard, or digital screen displaying match scores and remaining points.</li>
<li><strong>Set:</strong> A collection of legs (usually best of five) forming a unit of scoring in set-play championship matches.</li>
<li><strong>Setup Shot:</strong> A tactical visit made when out of checkout range, designed to leave an ideal, comfortable double for the following visit (e.g., scoring 60 from 92 to leave Double 16).</li>
<li><strong>Shaft (Stem):</strong> The rear component screwed into the barrel that secures the flight in place.</li>
<li><strong>Shanghai:</strong> A finish achieved by hitting a single, double, and treble of the same numerical bed in three darts (classic Shanghai is 120 on the 20s). Discover how it works in our <a href="/darts/shanghai-finish-in-darts/">Shanghai Finish Guide</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Sid Waddell Trophy:</strong> The prestigious trophy awarded annually to the PDC World Darts Champion, honoring the legendary commentator.</li>
<li><strong>Single:</strong> The large open sections of a segment, counting as the face value of the number (1 through 20).</li>
<li><strong>Single Bull:</strong> The outer green ring around the Bullseye, worth 25 points.</li>
<li><strong>Sisal:</strong> The natural plant fiber harvested from agave leaves, compressed under heavy hydraulic pressure to construct bristle dartboards.</li>
<li><strong>Spider:</strong> The thin metallic wire grid that delineates the scoring segments and rings on a dartboard.</li>
<li><strong>Stem:</strong> Synonymous with <em>Shaft</em>; the threaded piece connecting the barrel to the flight.</li>
<li><strong>Straight-In:</strong> A game format where scoring commences immediately on any bed without requiring an opening double.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter T</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-t"></a><strong>Table (Checkout Table):</strong> The mathematical matrix displaying recommended combination checkouts for every number from 2 to 170. Check our complete <a href="/darts/checkout-chart/">Checkout Chart Table</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Three in a Bed:</strong> Landing all three darts of a turn in the exact same scoring bed.</li>
<li><strong>Toe Line:</strong> Another term for the oche throw line.</li>
<li><strong>Ton:</strong> Darts terminology for a score of exactly <strong>100 points</strong> in a single visit.</li>
<li><strong>Ton-Eighty (180):</strong> The maximum three-dart score achievable, famously called as <em>"ONE HUNDRED AND EIGHTY!"</em>.</li>
<li><strong>Ton-Forty (140):</strong> Scoring 140 points in a turn, typically two Treble 20s and a Single 20.</li>
<li><strong>Tops:</strong> Universal darts slang for <strong>Double 20</strong> (worth 40 points), situated at the apex of the board.</li>
<li><strong>Tour Card:</strong> The professional playing credential held by the 128 players entitled to compete on the PDC ProTour circuit.</li>
<li><strong>Treble (Triple):</strong> The narrow inner ring on the board, measuring 8mm wide, which triples the numerical point value of the segment (e.g., Treble 20 = 60 points).</li>
<li><strong>Tungsten:</strong> The dense, heavy metal alloyed with nickel to manufacture professional dart barrels with slim, streamlined profiles.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter U</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-u"></a><strong>Unfinished:</strong> A player who has not yet checked out their remaining score to conclude the leg.</li>
<li><strong>Upstairs:</strong> Broadcast slang referring to the upper half of the board, primarily the <strong>Treble 20</strong> bed.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter V</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-v"></a><strong>Visit:</strong> A player's single turn at the oche, throwing up to three darts before relinquishing the stage to their opponent.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter W</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-w"></a><strong>Walk-On:</strong> The music-accompanied stage entrance made by competitors walking through the arena crowd. Browse the full playlist in our <a href="/players/walk-on-songs/">PDC Walk-On Songs Directory</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Wiring (Wire):</strong> When a dart strikes the metal dividing wire instead of penetrating cleanly into the sisal bed, often bouncing out or deflecting away.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter X</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-x"></a><strong>X-Bull:</strong> Occasional pub shorthand for the central 50-point double bullseye.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter Y</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-y"></a><strong>Yips:</strong> Another term for the involuntary throwing hesitation or motor disruption known in darts as <em>dartitis</em>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Letter Z</h3>
<ul>
<li><a id="letter-z"></a><strong>Zero Score (Bust):</strong> The result of an invalid finishing attempt where a player scores too many points or leaves a remainder of 1, resetting their turn to zero points scored.</li>
</ul>
      </section>
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Scoring terms</h2>
<p>In competitive 501, scoring revolves around maximizing points per turn. Rather than aiming at the center bullseye, professionals focus almost exclusively on the <strong>Treble 20</strong> bed—worth 60 points per dart.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>180 (Maximum):</strong> The pinnacle scoring visit in darts, achieved by grouping three darts into the 8mm-wide Treble 20 slot. <a href="/darts/what-is-a-180-in-darts/">Read the complete 180 Maximum Guide</a></li>
<li><strong>Ton &amp; Ton-40:</strong> Traditional terminology for a score of 100 points (<em>Ton</em>) or 140 points (<em>Ton-Forty</em>).</li>
<li><strong>Downstairs Switching:</strong> Moving from the Treble 20 bed down to <strong>Treble 19</strong> (57 points) or <strong>Treble 18</strong> (54 points) when the top bed is visually blocked.</li>
<li><strong>Three in a Bed:</strong> Landing all three darts of a turn into the exact same numerical segment.</li>
<li><strong>First-9 Average:</strong> The statistical scoring average across a player's opening nine darts of a leg, measuring pure scoring power before finishing doubles affect the number. <a href="/darts/average-explained/">Read the 3-Dart Average Guide</a></li>
</ul>
      </section>
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Checkout &amp; finish terms</h2>
<p>Finishing requires navigating subtraction combinations under intense pressure, always concluding on an outer double or the 50-point Bullseye.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The Big Fish (170):</strong> The highest legal checkout in darts (Treble 20, Treble 20, Bullseye). <a href="/darts/why-is-170-the-highest-checkout/">Read the 170 Highest Checkout Guide</a></li>
<li><strong>Shanghai Finish:</strong> Hitting the single, double, and treble of the same numerical bed in three darts (classic Shanghai is 120 on the 20s). <a href="/darts/shanghai-finish-in-darts/">Read the Shanghai Finish Guide</a></li>
<li><strong>Bogey Numbers:</strong> Seven mathematical totals below 170 (169, 168, 166, 165, 163, 162, 159) that cannot be completed in a single three-dart turn. <a href="/darts/bogey-numbers-in-darts/">Read the Bogey Numbers Guide</a></li>
<li><strong>Tops (Double 20):</strong> The top double on the board and the most popular professional finish target.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/darts/madhouse-in-darts/">The Madhouse (Double 1):</a></strong> The lowest double on the board, notoriously difficult because an inside miss results in a bust.</li>
<li><strong>Checkout Chart:</strong> The strategic table mapping out optimal routes from 170 down to 2. <a href="/darts/checkout-chart/">Consult the PDC Checkout Chart</a></li>
</ul>
      </section>
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Match-format terms (legs, sets, double-in)</h2>
<p>Tournament formats dictate match endurance, tactical pacing, and scoring rules sanctioned by the Darts Regulation Authority (DRA).</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Legs vs Sets:</strong> In legs-only play, the first player to a target number of legs wins; in set play (used at the World Championship), players must win three legs to take a set. <a href="/darts/legs-vs-sets/">Read the Legs vs Sets Guide</a></li>
<li><strong>Double-In Double-Out (DIDO):</strong> A demanding format where a player must hit a double to begin scoring in a leg and a double to finish (standard at the World Grand Prix). <a href="/darts/double-in-double-out/">Read the Double-In Double-Out Guide</a></li>
<li><strong>Bust Rule:</strong> Exceeding your remaining score, leaving 1, or hitting zero without a double voids the current visit and resets your score. <a href="/darts/bust-rule-in-darts/">Read the Bust Rule Guide</a></li>
<li><strong>The Oche:</strong> The raised 7ft 9¼in throw line behind which competitors must stand. <a href="/darts/board-height-and-distance/">Read the Board Height &amp; Distance Guide</a></li>
<li><strong>Break of Throw:</strong> Winning a leg started by your opponent, securing an advantage similar to a break of serve in tennis.</li>
</ul>
      </section>
      <section class="content-card">
<h2>Slang &amp; fan terms</h2>
<p>Decades of tavern culture and arena entertainment have created a distinct darts vernacular used by callers, pundits, and fans.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Robin Hood:</strong> When a dart point embeds into the shaft or flight of an already thrown dart; scores zero points under DRA rules. <a href="/darts/robin-hood-in-darts/">Read the Robin Hood Guide</a></li>
<li><strong>Bed &amp; Breakfast (26):</strong> Classic British pub slang for scoring 26 (Single 20, Single 1, Single 5).</li>
<li><strong>Dartitis:</strong> A severe psychological block preventing a player from smoothly releasing the dart.</li>
<li><strong>Ally Pally:</strong> The affectionate fan nickname for Alexandra Palace, venue of the PDC World Darts Championship.</li>
<li><strong>Walk-On:</strong> The music-fueled entrance of players to the arena stage. <a href="/players/walk-on-songs/">Explore the PDC Walk-On Anthems Directory</a></li>
</ul>
      </section>

      <section class="content-card faq-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What does "Madhouse" mean in darts?</h3>
            <p>In darts, <strong>"The Madhouse"</strong> is slang for <strong>Double 1</strong> (worth 2 points). It earned its reputation because once a player is on 2, any single 1 or miss inside busts the score to zero, leaving them trapped in a mental and mathematical loop that is notoriously difficult to escape.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Why is 170 called "The Big Fish"?</h3>
            <p>A checkout of <strong>170</strong> (Treble 20, Treble 20, Bullseye) is called <strong>"The Big Fish"</strong> because it represents the highest, most prized catch possible in the sport. It is the absolute mathematical maximum finish in 501 darts and requires nerves of steel to land the final Bullseye.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Does a "Robin Hood" count for points in darts?</h3>
            <p><strong>No.</strong> Under official Darts Regulation Authority (DRA) rules, a dart must have its point touching the bristle surface of the dartboard to score. A dart stuck into the flight or shaft of another dart scores <strong>zero points</strong>, even if the first dart is in a treble or bullseye.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What are "Bogey Numbers" in darts?</h3>
            <p>Bogey numbers are scores of 169 or lower that cannot be checked out with three darts under standard 501 rules: <strong>169, 168, 166, 165, 163, 162, and 159</strong>. Regardless of where a player aims, there is no mathematical combination of two trebles and a double that equals these numbers.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Why is the throw line called the "Oche"?</h3>
            <p>The word <strong>"Oche"</strong> (pronounced <em>ockey</em>) derives from early 20th-century English pub dialect. While an urban legend claims it was named after a brewery called "S. Hockey &amp; Sons", historical lexicography traces the term to the Old French word <em>oche</em>, meaning a notch, nick, or groove cut into tavern floorboards to designate the legal throwing mark.</p>
          </div>
      </section>

